    16	# Design analysis for the combo variant
    17	# -------------------------------------
    18	# a) Concrete behavior trace
    19	#    - Original task: input explicitly shows a black n x n mover on the blue river
    20	#      plus a red n x n destination. The solver starts BFS directly from the black
    21	#      square and can leave in any feasible direction.
    22	#    - D20 side of the combo: the black mover disappears from the input. Instead,
    23	#      one yellow border pixel marks the hidden source pocket:
    24	#        top    (0, c)   -> hidden start at (1, c)
    25	#        bottom (H-1, c) -> hidden start at (H-n-1, c)
    26	#        left   (r, 0)   -> hidden start at (r, 1)
    27	#        right  (r, W-1) -> hidden start at (r, W-n-1)
    28	#    - D18 side of the combo: a red border singleton separately tells the solver
    29	#      which first step the hidden mover must take:
    30	#        top border red pixel    -> first step down
    31	#        bottom border red pixel -> first step up
    32	#        left border red pixel   -> first step right
    33	#        right border red pixel  -> first step left
    34	#    - Combo behavior on a small example:
    35	#      1. Yellow pixel on the top edge says "the mover actually starts at row 1".
    36	#      2. Around that hidden pocket, the river visibly has two exits: a main route
    37	#         and a short dead-end stub.
    38	#      3. A red singleton on the right edge says "the first move must be left".
    39	#      4. The solver takes exactly that first move, removes the hidden start from
    40	#         the feasible placement graph, and therefore cannot come back to switch
    41	#         onto the other branch later.
    42	#      5. Output draws the black square only at its final resting place; the yellow
    43	#         marker and red control pixel remain as input annotations.
    44	#
    45	# b) Complexity calibration
    46	#    - The combo adds exactly two one-pixel annotations:
    47	#      one yellow source marker and one red direction marker.
    48	#    - The destination remains the only n x n red square; the mover never appears
    49	#      in the input. This keeps roles visually distinct without introducing a full
    50	#      legend table.
    51	#    - n is restricted to 2..4. This prevents the red destination from collapsing
    52	#      into a 1 x 1 red pixel that would be ambiguous with the red direction marker.
    53	#
    54	# c) Implicit assumption audit of the original solver
    55	#    - "There are at least two visible same-size squares" breaks: the mover is now
    56	#      hidden, so the solver cannot sort visible squares by color anymore.
    57	#    - "Red uniquely means destination" breaks: red now appears as both an n x n
    58	#      target square and a border singleton. The solver must distinguish them by
    59	#      size and border placement, not by raw color alone.
    60	#    - "BFS may leave the start through any feasible neighbor" breaks: the combo
    61	#      requires a decoded first step and forbids returning through the hidden start.
    62	#    - "The input already contains mover-colored traversable cells" breaks: the
    63	#      hidden start pocket is blue river, so traversability should use river+dest.
    64	#
    65	# d) Placement legality requirements enforced by the generator
    66	#    - The yellow source marker is always on a non-corner border cell and decodes
    67	#      to a legal hidden n x n source pocket one cell inside the grid.
    68	#    - The red direction marker is also on a non-corner border cell, never merges
    69	#      4-neighbor-wise with the red destination square, and never overlaps the
    70	#      yellow source marker.
    71	#    - The hidden source pocket, river, destination, and optional chokepoints are
    72	#      all built before overlaying markers so that the decoded state is legal.
    73	#
    74	# e) Visual observability
    75	#    - Across samples, humans can see that:
    76	#      1. the black square is absent from every input,
    77	#      2. a yellow edge pixel consistently marks where the black square appears from,
    78	#      3. a red edge singleton separately determines which visible branch it takes.
    79	#    - The dead-end stub near the hidden start makes the red direction control
    80	#      visibly consequential instead of decorative.
    81	#
    82	# f) Visual cleanliness
    83	#    - Both annotations live on the border, outside the busy channel interior.
    84	#    - Only one extra dead-end stub is added near the hidden start; the rest of the
    85	#      river keeps the original task's low-entropy winding appearance.
    86	#
    87	# g) Inferability of changes
    88	#    - Color usage stays low-entropy:
    89	#      green background, blue river, red target + direction control, yellow source
    90	#      marker, black mover only in the output.
    91	#    - The two border annotations have different structural roles:
    92	#      yellow decodes location, red decodes direction. Because the output changes
    93	#      when the chosen branch changes, that two-step reading process is inferable.
    94	#
    95	# h) Anti-degeneration target
    96	#    - A valid generated sample must satisfy all of:
    97	#      1. zero black pixels in the input,
    98	#      2. exactly one yellow border source marker,
    99	#      3. exactly one red border singleton separate from the red n x n target,
   100	#      4. the chosen first-step branch ends somewhere different from the result of
